    "3.0",
    "A WIOT file is a file that has been renamed and encrypted by WIOT ransomware, which is malware distributed by cybercriminals. It contains a normal file, such as a document, song, video, or image, that the ransomware is holding hostage. WIOT files cannot be decrypted. In September 2021, some PC users began reporting their computers had been infected by a new variant of STOP ransomware. This variant encrypts users files and appends them with the wiot extension. The ransomware then produces a plain text ransom note named _readme.txt in every folder that contains an infected file. The note contains instructions users are supposed to follow to decrypt their files. How did my computer get infected by WIOT ransomware? WIOT ransomware is most commonly distributed in programs that appear to be legitimate but are actually adware and malware bundles. If you recently downloaded and installed an unverified program, that may be how WIOT ransomware infected your computer. NOTE: To learn more about STOP ransomware and its variants, refer to the.STOP entry. Because WIOT files are encrypted, you cannot open them. Additionally, because WIOT files are encrypted using an online private key, there is no way to decrypt them. If your computer has been infected by WIOT ransomware, the best option to restore your files to their original state is likely to perform a System Restore. Note that performing a System Restore will cause you to lose any data created in the interval between now and your last restore point. So, you may want to back up any data and files created since your last restore point. No programs available.",
